-- title:
--   Component charts and attached annuli at a supersingular crossing
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, a level $N$ with $q \nmid N$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$ whose vanishing locus is exactly the maximal ideal of $A$, modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \equiv (X^q - Y)(X - Y^q) \bmod q$, integrality hypotheses `hα`, `hβ` for the two degeneracy embeddings of Laurent-series function fields, and a place specialisation $P$ for these data. Let $W$ be a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` consisting precisely of the supersingular places (rational, affine for both $j$-generators, with $j$-value in the supersingular set), and let $R$ be a prolongation tuple over $P$ satisfying the model laws `IsModel` (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws), the regularity and node-value laws over $W$, and the fixed-order law. Fix a number field $K \subset \overline{\mathbb Q}$, a place $w \in W$ at which $R$ satisfies the value-integrality law, and a crossing presentation at $w$: an element $\varpi$ of the coefficient subring $A \cap K$ whose multiples are exactly the kernel of the reduction restricted to that subring, an exponent $e_K \ge 1$ and a unit $\varepsilon$ with $q = \varpi^{e_K}\varepsilon$, node coordinates $c = (x,y)$ over $K$ at $w$ (so $x$ reduces to zero on the first branch and its second residue has order $1$ at the Frobenius twist of $w$, while $y$ reduces to zero on the second branch and its first residue has order $1$ at $w$), an exponent $e_w \ge 1$ and a unit $u$ of the ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K\,w$ with $xy = c_w(\varpi)^{e_w e_K}u$, the hypotheses that the ideal generated by $c_w(\varpi), x, y$ is the unique maximal ideal, that $(c_w(\varpi),x)$ and $(c_w(\varpi),y)$ are prime with $y$ outside the first and $x$ outside the second, that the ring is Noetherian, and that every element differs from some constant $c_w(o)$ by a non-unit. Finally let $\mu$ be an absolute value on $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $A = \{\mu \le 1\}$. Then, with `ResidueField A` acting on `modularFunctionFieldC k N` through $R.\mathrm{redBar}$ followed by the structure map of $k$, there exist two component charts $C_1, C_2$ for $A$ on `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` with values in `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, nodes $x_1, x_2$ of the latter over the residue field of $A$, and two annuli $\mathrm{An}_1, \mathrm{An}_2$ such that: $\mathrm{An}_2$ and $\mathrm{An}_1$ have the same domain and the same nonzero modulus, the product of their parameters is the image of that modulus, $\mathrm{An}_1$ is attached to $(C_1,x_1)$ and $\mathrm{An}_2$ to $(C_2,x_2)$, two places of the common domain give different $\mu$-values to the parameter of $\mathrm{An}_1$; the valuation subrings of $C_1$ and $C_2$ coincide with those of $R.R_1$ and $R.R_2$, and for every $f$ in them the chart residue vanishes exactly when the prolongation residue does, with $\mathrm{ord}_{x_1}(C_1\text{-residue}) = \mathrm{ord}_w(R.\mathrm{residue}_1)$ and $\mathrm{ord}_{x_2}(C_2\text{-residue}) = \mathrm{ord}_{\mathrm{arithFrob}\cdot w}(R.\mathrm{residue}_2)$; the domain of $\mathrm{An}_1$ consists exactly of the places $V$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V = w$ that are strict of neither kind; on that domain $\mu(V(\mathrm{param})) = \mu(V(y))$; and $\mu(\mathrm{modulus}) = \mu(q)^{e_w}$.
--
--   This is the instantiation step that produces, at a single supersingular crossing of $X_0(Nq)$, the semistable chart-and-annulus data (two component charts meeting at a node, joined by a pair of annuli whose parameters multiply to a modulus of absolute value $|q|^{e_w}$) out of the abstract crossing presentation of the local ring at the node. It is used by [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.valuation_pow_le_mul_prod_and_rigid_of_twist`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.valuation_pow_le_mul_prod_and_rigid_of_twist).
